generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\n**Cause:** Python can't store 2.675 exactly. The float it keeps is 2.67499999\u2026, so `round(x, 2)` rounds it down. `round()` also rounds exact halves to the nearest even digit, which made `0.125` come out as `0.12` instead of `0.13`. Both broke the function's own docstring, which promises half-up rounding.\n\n**Fix (`pricing/money.py`):** `round_money` now converts the number to the short decimal form Python prints for it (`2.675`), then rounds to cents with `Decimal.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), ROUND_HALF_UP)`. It still returns a float, so `add_vat` and other callers work unchanged.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_money.py`, which checks `2.675\u21922.68`, `1.005\u21921.01`, `0.125\u21920.13`, `2.674\u21922.67` and `-2.675\u2192-2.68` (negative halves round away from zero). The old `round()` gives `2.67 1.0 0.12` for the first three, so the test fails without the fix. pytest isn't installed here, so I called the test functions directly with `python3`. The new test and the existing `test_split` both pass.\n\n**Not fixed:** `split()` has a similar float problem. `int(total * 100)` cuts off the decimals, so an amount like `0.29` can become 28 cents instead of 29. I didn't change it because it wasn't part of this request.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 9, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 253750, "requests_cache_write": 8564, "requests_cost_usd": 0.13765, "requests_count": 9, "requests_input_uncached": 20, "requests_output": 2200, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial8", "run_seed": 20261014, "sampling_observed": null, "schedule_index": 15, "score": 1.0, "started_at": "2026-10-13T06:08:42.153943+00:00", "subagent_resumed": 